ight absolutely so you know  we think this pneumonia basically has  two phases this silent hypoxia period  where the oxygen is drifting down what  you feel okay then the second part of  this disease the part of this disease  that kills is when all of a sudden your  lungs get stiff carbon dioxide's finally  starts to build up you begin to feel  short of breath and those patients are  the patients who are presenting with  Kovach pneumonia by then in some cases  it's too late by the time you go to the  hospital and you can't breathe it's a  very serious often dire situation yeah  but let me just reassure people that  what the lessons learned in New York are  are that we don't have to put in  breathing tubes even in most of the  people who show up with advanced COBIT  pneumonia we don't we know they did a  study of 50 patients and what we learned  is that even people who showed up with  significant coke with pneumonia we could  keep off of ventilators three out of  four of them so 75% of patients didn't  need ventilators what they needed oxygen  they needed to do what we call  positioning maneuvers so we put them in  different positions to help open up  areas of their lungs  how do you know dr. Leavitt and when you  should even check your oxygen levels  let's talk about Germany the Germans  have the lowest death rates in Europe  what they do is every patient and who  has COBIT they visit every day and they  check their oxygen and they check their  vital signs and so what I'm saying to  you is if you're having symptoms of  viral illness if you're known to have  Kovac you should be checking your oxygen  if you don't have an oximeter increased  breathing may be a sign of that but also  just feeling worse most people listening  to you will now think I need to get an  oximeter can you explain exactly what  that is and how it works basically you  know you just turn this little button on  and you put it on your finger and within  about 10 seconds or so it displays your  heart 